15-5f84-b5da-4675f952102f"]] "retractions" [[17592186272045 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-22x+121 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-22x+{11}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 11x+{11}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-11{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-22x+121)-4 & = \\\\ (x-11{)}^{2}-4 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186271768 :answer-field/correct 17592186269568] [17592186271768 :answer-field/choices 17592186269568] [17592186271768 :answer-field/choices 17592186270849] [17592186271768 :answer-field/choices 17592186270659] [17592186271768 :answer-field/choices 17592186270793] [17592186271768 :answer-field/choices 17592186269356] [17592186272296 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 5x+16\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $16$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+16\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+16\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+5{)}^{2}-9.\n$$"] [17592186271540 :answer-field/correct 17592186269789] [17592186271540 :answer-field/choices 17592186270456] [17592186271540 :answer-field/choices 17592186269522] [17592186271540 :answer-field/choices 17592186269432] [17592186271540 :answer-field/choices 17592186271177] [17592186271540 :answer-field/choices 17592186269789]]}]